course: dictionary
lesson: 5234
title: A word from the water's edge
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A jetty is a place where boats stop by the water. The dictionary says it is a small wooden or stone platform built out into the sea, a lake or a river. Read the words around it to find its meaning. «Step off carefully with a hand on the jetty» tells you it is something solid you can hold, next to the boat.
explain: Jetty can also mean a wall built out into the sea to protect a harbour or beach from waves. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence. When you read «your legs push the boat backwards, so it drifts away», you know the jetty is by the water, so the first meaning fits best.
explain: Check with the swap trick. Replace jetty with "the platform by the water" and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, your meaning is right. A boat drifts away when it is pushed backwards, which is why you need a hand on something that stays still.
fact: A jetty is a small wooden or stone platform built out into water.
fact: «Jetty» can also mean a wall built out into the sea to protect a harbour or beach from waves.
fact: Boats tie up at the jetty, and children fish from it.
fact: «Step off carefully with a hand on the jetty» means hold on to the jetty so the boat does not drift away.
fact: Never run or jump off a jetty without checking how deep the water is.
exercise: «Step off carefully with a hand on the jetty.» What is a jetty? | A deep hole dug in the sand | *A small wooden or stone platform built out into the water | A tall tower on a hill
exercise: «Your legs push the boat backwards, so it drifts away.» Why should you put a hand on the jetty when you step off? | To paint the boat | To make the boat go faster | To wave to your friends | *To stop the boat drifting away
exercise: Where do boats tie up? | In the sky | Under the desk | *At the jetty | Inside a castle
test: Jetty can mean a wall built out into the sea. What does that wall protect? | The sky from rain | *A harbour or beach from waves | A castle from dragons
test: Swap the word in «Step off carefully with a hand on the platform by the water.» Does it still make sense? | No, because platforms cannot be by water | *Yes, so the platform by the water is a good meaning for jetty | No, because hands cannot touch platforms
exam: Which sentence best shows that a jetty is next to water? | Her jetty was very quiet and calm | *Boats tie up at the jetty and children fish from it | The jetty was painted red on Monday | The jetty is a kind of cake
